## Domain 1: Agents and Workflows (Q1.1–Q1.9)

### Q1.1 · Agent Architecture
A meeting-scheduling assistant runs a fixed sequence for the common case — check the
requester's calendar, check the invitee's calendar, propose the first mutual opening.
Occasionally a request involves five people across three time zones with no easy
mutual opening, requiring genuine tradeoff exploration (who can move, which
constraints are firm) before a workable time can even be proposed.

A) A pure fixed workflow for every scheduling request, since most requests are
   two-person and simple, and the five-person case can simply be told there's no
   available time.
B) A hybrid: the fixed sequence handles the common two-party case end to end, with a
   branch to agentic, exploratory reasoning specifically for the multi-party,
   no-easy-overlap case a fixed sequence can't represent in advance.
C) A pure autonomous agent for every scheduling request, since any request could in
   principle turn out to need multi-party negotiation.
D) A supervisor with one subagent per invitee, since decomposing by person always
   improves scheduling reliability regardless of how many people are actually
   involved.

### Q1.2 · Agent Architecture
A supervisor agent sits in front of specialized billing, technical, and legal-review
subagents, but its own reasoning increasingly tries to answer legal-review questions
directly using general knowledge rather than routing them to the legal-review
specialist, reasoning "I can probably answer this well enough myself."

A) This is fine, since a capable supervisor should always prefer answering directly
   over the overhead of delegating, regardless of whether a specialist exists for the
   domain.
B) This undermines the purpose of having a legal-review specialist in the first place:
   a supervisor should route a request to the specialist actually suited to it rather
   than substitute its own general reasoning for a domain the system was specifically
   built to hand off, especially where specialist review exists for a reason (accuracy,
   compliance, or scope of expertise).
C) This is fine as long as the supervisor's answer happens to be correct most of the
   time, since occasional correctness is the only bar delegation decisions need to
   meet.
D) This is a Claude Hooks problem and should be fixed with a `PostToolUse` hook that
   corrects the supervisor's answer after the fact rather than by changing how routing
   decisions are made.

### Q1.3 · Agent Architecture
A monitoring assistant needs to check the health of ten independent internal services,
none of which depends on any other's result. The team implements this as ten
sequential tool calls, one per turn, each waiting for the previous to finish before
issuing the next.

A) This should be restructured as ten separate subagents delegated via `Task`, since
   parallelizing independent work is inherently a subagent-delegation problem
   regardless of what else is available.
B) Since the ten checks are independent and don't depend on each other's results, the
   model should be encouraged to issue all ten as parallel `tool_use` blocks within a
   single turn, letting the harness execute them concurrently — subagent delegation
   would add context-loading overhead here for no benefit over plain parallel tool
   calls to the same kind of check.
C) The current sequential approach is optimal, since executing checks one at a time
   guarantees no service is checked before the previous one's result is fully
   processed.
D) The fix is to combine all ten checks into a single custom tool that checks every
   service internally, since this is strictly better than either parallel tool calls
   or subagent delegation in every case.

### Q1.4 · Agent Construction with Claude
A finance-ops agent on the Claude Agent SDK is bounded by `max_budget_usd`, which
terminates the run outright once the threshold is hit — including mid-task, with no
opportunity for a human to review what's happened so far and decide whether to
authorize continuing.

A) A hard termination at the budget threshold is always the correct behavior, since
   any spend beyond a pre-set limit should end the task immediately with no exception
   under any circumstance.
B) For a task where a human might reasonably want to authorize continuing past the
   threshold rather than lose all progress, a checkpoint-and-ask pattern (pause at the
   threshold, surface what's been done so far, and let a human decide whether to
   authorize more budget) is a more appropriate design than an unconditional hard
   stop — the two mechanisms serve different needs, and a hard budget cap alone
   doesn't provide the "ask before continuing" option this scenario calls for.
C) The fix is to remove `max_budget_usd` entirely, since any spending limit
   inherently conflicts with allowing a human to authorize continued work.
D) The fix is to raise `max_budget_usd` to an arbitrarily high value, since a higher
   threshold is functionally equivalent to giving a human the option to authorize
   more spend.

### Q1.5 · Agent Construction with Claude
A developer bounces between three unrelated tasks within one long-lived Claude Code
session over the course of a day — fixing a bug, reviewing an unrelated PR, then
writing documentation for a third feature — and finds that by the afternoon, stale
context from the morning's bug investigation is bleeding into unrelated later work.

A) Starting an entirely new session at every task boundary is the only way to prevent
   this, discarding everything learned earlier in the day, including genuinely useful
   project conventions picked up during the morning's work.
B) Using compaction at each task boundary — condensing the resolved task into a
   summary before moving to the next — lets decisions and conventions worth keeping
   survive while the noisy, resolved-task detail stops crowding out and contaminating
   the next unrelated task.
C) Increasing the context window is the correct fix, since a larger window would let
   all three days' worth of task detail coexist without the model needing to choose
   what's still relevant.
D) `context: fork` should be used for each of the three tasks regardless of whether
   they're structured as delegated, hand-off-and-return units, since forking always
   isolates context regardless of what the parent session is doing.

### Q1.6 · Agent Construction with Claude
A research team needs to run extensive custom fine-tuning experiments on model weights
and requires direct, low-level access to modify and inspect the model beyond what any
hosted API — managed or self-hosted-via-cloud-platform — is designed to expose.

A) An Anthropic-hosted managed deployment is sufficient here, since managed offerings
   always include full weight-level access and fine-tuning capability by default
   regardless of the specific research need.
B) This need genuinely falls outside what either managed or typical self-hosted API
   deployment models are designed to provide — those models are built around calling
   a served model via an API, not around direct weight-level modification — so the
   team should recognize this as a different kind of requirement than a standard
   Claude deployment decision addresses, rather than forcing it into either standard
   option.
C) A self-hosted deployment via a cloud platform is always sufficient for any
   weight-level research need, since self-hosting is defined as having unrestricted
   access to model internals.
D) Neither deployment model matters for this use case, since fine-tuning research has
   no meaningful relationship to how an application-facing deployment is configured.

### Q1.7 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A workflow needs a human to explicitly approve a step midway through an otherwise
automated multi-stage process (e.g., approving a draft before it's sent), with the
flow able to pause and later resume at exactly that point. A separate, much simpler
workflow just needs to send a fixed notification after a single trigger event, with no
branching or pauses of any kind.

A) A graph-based framework with support for pause/resume and human-in-the-loop nodes
   is a reasonable fit for the approval workflow's genuine need to stop mid-process
   and wait on external input; the simple single-trigger notification doesn't need
   that machinery and is better served by a plain, direct implementation.
B) Both workflows should use the same graph-based framework uniformly, since having
   the capability to pause and resume is never a disadvantage even for a workflow that
   will never actually use it.
C) Neither workflow benefits from any framework, since human-in-the-loop approval can
   only be implemented through fully custom code with no reusable pattern.
D) The simple notification workflow needs the graph framework more, since sending a
   notification is itself a form of external interaction serious enough to warrant
   pause/resume support.

### Q1.8 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A team has an assistant persist "the user prefers vegetarian restaurant
recommendations" (a standing preference that should apply to every future
interaction) and also persist "the user is looking for a restaurant near the airport
today because their flight was delayed" (a fact relevant only to today's specific
request) — both saved identically to the same permanent, cross-session memory store
with no distinction between them.

A) This is correct, since anything a user says should be treated identically and
   persisted permanently regardless of whether it reflects a standing preference or a
   one-off situational detail.
B) These have different appropriate lifetimes: the vegetarian preference is durable
   and worth persisting across sessions, while the flight-delay/today-only detail is
   task-scoped and shouldn't be carried forward indefinitely as if it were a standing
   fact — treating both identically over-persists information that will become stale
   or actively misleading in future sessions.
C) Neither should be persisted at all, since any information mentioned in conversation
   is inherently too unreliable to store in external memory under any circumstance.
D) Both should be discarded at the end of the session and re-collected from the user
   every time, since re-asking is always more reliable than any persistence mechanism.

### Q1.9 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks *(select TWO)*
Which two statements accurately describe how memory and context-management patterns
relate to each other in a long-running agentic system?

A) A case-facts scratchpad protects specific, load-bearing findings from being lost to
   compaction or summarization, independent of whatever else happens to the
   surrounding raw context.
B) Delegating open-ended, expendable-context exploration to a subagent keeps that
   exploration's bulk out of the main session's context, distinct from — and
   complementary to — pruning or compacting content that's already accumulated there.
C) A sufficiently large context window removes any need for memory or context-
   management patterns altogether, since nothing would ever need to be summarized,
   pruned, or delegated once the window is big enough.
D) Once a fact is written to a case-facts scratchpad, it's guaranteed to remain
   accurate indefinitely with no possibility of becoming stale as a task evolves.
E) Persisted, cross-session memory and in-session context management (compaction,
   pruning, subagent isolation) address different problems — durability across
   sessions versus signal-to-noise within a single long session — rather than being
   two names for the same mechanism.

