## Domain 2: Applications and Integration (Q2.1–Q2.19)

### Q2.1 · Understanding Requirements
A retail requirements document contains: "the assistant shall recommend products based
on a customer's browsing history," "the recommendation service must support 10,000
concurrent requests at sub-200ms latency," and "the business wants to increase average
order value by 15% this year."

A) All three are functional requirements, since all three relate to the same
   recommendation feature.
B) The first is functional (a specific behavior), the second is an infrastructure
   requirement (a concurrency/latency constraint), and the third is a business goal
   the first two exist to help achieve — three genuinely distinct categories, not one
   collapsed into another.
C) The second and third are both business goals, since both relate to overall business
   performance rather than a single feature's behavior.
D) The first and second are both infrastructure requirements, since both directly
   affect the technical architecture of the recommendation service.

### Q2.2 · Understanding Requirements
A requirement is stated with a specific, testable target: "search results must return
within 800ms at the 95th percentile for queries under 50 characters." A team proposes
loosening it to "search results should return reasonably quickly" to "allow more
implementation flexibility."

A) Loosening the requirement is a genuine improvement, since more flexible wording
   always gives engineering more room to make good tradeoffs.
B) The original requirement's specificity is exactly what makes it testable and
   useful for verifying the system actually meets it; replacing it with vague language
   removes that property rather than adding flexibility — a well-formed, measurable
   requirement doesn't need to be loosened to be workable, and doing so trades away
   verifiability for the appearance of flexibility.
C) Neither version of the requirement is usable, since any requirement expressed as a
   single sentence is inherently too underspecified to build against.
D) The two versions are functionally equivalent, since both ultimately describe the
   same underlying intent regardless of wording.

### Q2.3 · Systems Life Cycle
An internal Claude-powered tool has been superseded by a newer system. The team is
migrating remaining users off it, archiving its conversation logs per a data-retention
policy, and formally decommissioning the infrastructure it ran on.

A) This is operations and maintenance, since decommissioning happens to infrastructure
   that was previously operated and maintained.
B) This is retirement/decommissioning — a distinct life-cycle phase from ongoing
   operations and maintenance, concerned specifically with winding a system down,
   migrating its users, and properly disposing of its data and infrastructure rather
   than keeping it running.
C) This is requirements analysis for the replacement system, since decommissioning the
   old system is a prerequisite for the new one's rollout.
D) This phase doesn't exist in a systems life cycle; once a system launches, it either
   remains in operations and maintenance indefinitely or is deleted with no formal
   phase in between.

### Q2.4 · Systems Life Cycle
A team patches a security vulnerability discovered in a third-party dependency used
by their Claude-integrated service, three months after the service's initial launch,
with no new features involved — purely a corrective fix to something already in
production.

A) This is implementation, since writing and applying a patch is technically still
   writing code.
B) This is a maintenance activity within the broader operations and maintenance
   phase — a corrective fix applied to a system that's already live, distinct from
   the routine monitoring/dashboard-watching side of operations, but still part of
   sustaining a system already in production rather than building or launching a new
   one.
C) This is requirements analysis, since discovering a vulnerability is a form of
   identifying a new requirement.
D) This is system design, since choosing how to patch the vulnerability involves a
   design decision.

### Q2.5 · Claude API Mechanics
A team needs to submit a native PDF document (not a scanned image) for analysis and
currently converts every PDF to a series of page images first, assuming Claude has no
way to accept PDF content directly.

A) This assumption is outdated for this use case: Claude supports native document
   content blocks for PDFs directly, without requiring a separate image-conversion
   step — the team's image-conversion workaround is unnecessary extra work for a
   capability that's natively supported.
B) The team's approach is correct and required, since Claude can only ever process
   image content and has no mechanism for accepting document-format input of any
   kind.
C) PDF support exists only through a separate, unrelated OCR product entirely outside
   the Claude API, so document content must always be pre-converted to text before
   being sent.
D) Native PDF support exists but only for single-page documents; any multi-page PDF
   must still be split into individual page images before submission.

### Q2.6 · Claude API Mechanics
A team estimates input token cost based only on the length of the user-facing message
text, and is surprised that actual input token usage runs consistently higher than
their estimate on every request that includes tool definitions.

A) This indicates a billing error, since only message content should ever count
   toward input token usage regardless of what else is included in the request.
B) Tool definitions (names, descriptions, and parameter schemas) supplied with a
   request contribute to input token count just as message content does; a cost
   estimate that only accounts for message text will systematically undercount actual
   usage on any request that also includes tool definitions.
C) Tool definitions only count toward token usage the first time they're sent in a
   session and are free on every subsequent request within the same session
   automatically.
D) Tool definitions count toward output tokens rather than input tokens, so the
   discrepancy must be located somewhere in the response rather than the request.

### Q2.7 · Claude API Mechanics
A team configures a custom stop sequence to end generation at a specific delimiter for
downstream parsing convenience, but the chosen sequence turns out to also appear
naturally inside legitimate content partway through several responses, causing those
responses to be cut off mid-thought with `stop_reason: "stop_sequence"` well before
the model would have naturally finished.

A) This is expected and cannot be avoided; any custom stop sequence will eventually
   match unintended content regardless of how it's chosen.
B) Checking `stop_reason` reveals the actual cause here — the custom stop sequence
   matched earlier than intended; choosing a stop sequence less likely to occur
   naturally within legitimate content (or removing it if no sequence can be chosen
   safely) addresses the false-early-termination problem, rather than treating every
   early stop as expected model behavior.
C) The fix is to ignore `stop_reason` entirely and always treat truncated output as a
   `max_tokens` issue, since that's the only stop condition genuinely worth handling.
D) The fix is to increase `max_tokens`, since a sequence match unrelated to output
   length can't be resolved by changing the output-length budget.

### Q2.8 · Claude API Mechanics
A team submits a request to the Message Batches API and, in their client code,
immediately attempts to read the completed results from the same call, exactly as
they would with a synchronous Messages API request — and is confused when no results
are available yet.

A) This indicates the Batches API is broken, since any API call should return its
   final result synchronously regardless of processing time.
B) The Batches API is asynchronous by design: submitting a batch returns a batch
   reference, and results must be retrieved separately once processing completes
   (via polling or a subsequent retrieval call) — treating batch submission as if it
   blocks until results are ready misunderstands the fundamental request/response
   pattern the Batches API uses.
C) The fix is to add a `stream: true` parameter to the batch submission, since
   streaming and synchronous batch completion are the same underlying mechanism.
D) Results are only retrievable from the Batches API within the same API call that
   submitted the batch, so a separate retrieval step is never required or possible.

### Q2.9 · Software Engineering Foundations
A user's request to a Claude-powered feature passes through four separate internal
microservices before a final response is returned. When something goes wrong,
engineers have to manually correlate timestamps across four separate services' logs
to reconstruct what happened to one specific request, since no shared identifier
threads through the whole call chain.

A) This is acceptable, since correlating logs by timestamp alone is a standard and
   sufficiently reliable way to trace a request across service boundaries.
B) Propagating a shared correlation/trace ID through every service in the call chain
   (distributed tracing) lets a specific request's full path be reconstructed
   directly, rather than relying on manual, error-prone timestamp correlation across
   independently-logged services.
C) The fix is to consolidate all four microservices into a single monolithic service,
   since distributed tracing is only ever needed as a workaround for having too many
   separate services.
D) Tracing across service boundaries is not possible for Claude-integrated services
   specifically, regardless of what tracing infrastructure the rest of the
   organization already has in place.

### Q2.10 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team performs a breaking schema change on the production database table storing
conversation history — dropping and recreating a column in place during a single
maintenance window with no backward-compatible rollout — causing a service outage
while older application instances (still running the previous code) fail against the
new schema before the deploy fully completes.

A) This is expected and cannot be avoided; any database schema change to a live table
   inherently requires an outage regardless of migration strategy.
B) A backward-compatible migration strategy (e.g., additive changes rolled out first,
   old and new code able to coexist against the schema during the transition, with
   the breaking cleanup deferred until all instances are on the new code) avoids
   forcing older, still-running instances to fail against a schema they weren't built
   for.
C) The fix is to avoid ever storing conversation history in a database at all, since
   any stateful storage is inherently prone to this class of issue.
D) The fix is to perform the exact same breaking migration, but during a lower-traffic
   time window, since timing alone fully resolves the compatibility issue regardless
   of migration strategy.

### Q2.11 · Software Engineering Foundations
A service's only dependency on the Claude API starts failing consistently due to an
extended outage. Instead of detecting the sustained failure and short-circuiting
further calls, the service keeps retrying every incoming request against the still-
failing dependency at full volume, adding retry traffic on top of an outage and
delaying the service's own recovery once the dependency comes back.

A) This is acceptable, since retrying every request individually is always the
   correct response to any dependency failure regardless of how long it persists.
B) A circuit-breaker pattern — detecting a sustained failure rate and "opening" to
   fail fast (or serve a fallback) for a period rather than continuing to hammer a
   dependency that's already down — reduces load on a struggling dependency and lets
   the service recover its own resources, rather than compounding an outage with
   continued full-volume retry traffic.
C) The fix is to increase the retry count per request even further, since more
   attempts per request will eventually succeed once the dependency recovers.
D) The fix is to remove all error handling for this dependency entirely, since any
   handling logic is itself adding unnecessary overhead during an outage.

### Q2.12 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team's Claude-integration layer has no README, runbook, or documented explanation of
how the system prompt is assembled, which tools are registered, or what to check
first during an incident — all of that knowledge exists only in the heads of the two
engineers who originally built it, neither of whom is on call the night a
production incident occurs.

A) This is an acceptable risk, since documentation for an internal integration layer
   is a lower priority than shipping new features, regardless of on-call coverage.
B) A basic README and incident runbook — how the system prompt and tools are
   assembled, what a healthy state looks like, and what to check first during an
   incident — is foundational engineering practice for any component someone besides
   its original authors may need to operate or debug under time pressure.
C) The fix is to require the two original engineers to be on call for this system
   indefinitely, since documentation is an inadequate substitute for direct original
   author knowledge.
D) Documentation has no bearing on incident response time, since an on-call engineer
   should be able to reconstruct system behavior from source code alone regardless of
   how well-documented it is.

### Q2.13 · Claude Application Design
A product expanding into new markets currently hardcodes English-only prompt
templates and output-formatting assumptions (date formats, currency symbols, tone
conventions), and localization is handled, after the fact, by manually translating
whatever English output the model happens to produce.

A) This is sufficient, since translating output after generation is always equivalent
   in quality to generating output natively suited to the target locale from the
   start.
B) Designing for localization from the start (locale-aware prompt templates, format
   conventions, and instructions that vary by target market) produces more
   appropriate output for each locale than bolting translation onto English-only
   output after the fact, and avoids baking English-specific assumptions into the
   application's core design.
C) Localization is purely a translation-layer concern with no implications for prompt
   or application design, so the current approach requires no architectural change.
D) The fix is to build an entirely separate application per target market, since
   locale-aware design within a single application is not achievable.

### Q2.14 · Claude Application Design
A chat application lets users regenerate an assistant's response, but the current
implementation simply overwrites the prior response in a single linear history,
losing the original response entirely and occasionally corrupting downstream
`tool_use`/`tool_result` pairing when a regenerated turn's tool calls don't match
what the overwritten turn's history expected.

A) This is fine, since users are unlikely to want to see a discarded response again
   once they've asked for a regeneration.
B) Supporting regeneration well requires managing multiple candidate branches of
   conversation history explicitly (rather than overwriting in place), ensuring each
   branch's `tool_use`/`tool_result` pairs stay consistent within that branch — a
   naive overwrite risks exactly the kind of orphaned or mismatched tool-call state
   described here.
C) The fix is to disable the regenerate feature entirely, since branching
   conversation state is not something a chat application can support.
D) The fix is to always discard all tool calls whenever a user regenerates a response,
   regardless of whether the current turn actually depends on them.

### Q2.15 · Claude Application Design
A feature that summarizes a user's private uploaded documents by sending them to
Claude is enabled by default for every user, with no explicit disclosure or consent
step informing users that their document content will be sent to a third-party model
provider for processing.

A) This is acceptable, since summarization is a helpful feature and users would
   presumably consent if asked, so an explicit consent step adds friction without
   meaningfully changing the outcome.
B) A feature that sends private user data to an external processing service warrants
   explicit disclosure and, where required, opt-in consent — enabling this by default
   without informing users bypasses a design consideration that matters for privacy
   and trust, independent of whether users would likely have said yes if asked.
C) Consent is only relevant for features involving payment information, so a
   document-summarization feature has no meaningful consent consideration to design
   for.
D) The fix is to process all documents locally without ever calling Claude, since no
   design short of eliminating the external call can adequately address the consent
   question.

### Q2.16 · Claude Application Design
A chat application has no protection against a user double-tapping "send" on a slow
connection, resulting in the same message being submitted twice and two separate
assistant responses being generated and shown for what the user intended as one
message.

A) This is acceptable, since duplicate responses are a minor cosmetic issue not worth
   addressing at the application layer.
B) Client-side design (disabling the send action while a request is in flight,
   and/or de-duplicating rapid identical submissions) directly prevents this specific
   failure mode at the point it originates, rather than only handling it as a backend
   concern after two full requests have already been made.
C) The fix belongs exclusively on the backend via an idempotency key, since
   client-side UI state has no bearing on whether a duplicate request gets sent in
   the first place.
D) The fix is to slow down the send button's response time deliberately, since a
   slower UI reduces the likelihood of accidental double-taps regardless of
   connection speed.

### Q2.17 · Claude Application Design
An agent has both fast tools (an inventory lookup that returns in under a second) and
a slow tool (a report-generation call that can take several minutes), but the chat UI
blocks entirely — showing no feedback — for the full duration of any tool call,
identical to how it behaves for the fast ones.

A) This is fine, since users should expect to wait for any tool call regardless of how
   long it takes, and a uniform blocking experience is simplest to implement.
B) Long-running tool calls warrant a different UX treatment than fast ones — surfacing
   an "in progress" state and allowing the interface to remain responsive (or polling
   for completion) rather than blocking identically to a sub-second lookup — designing
   for the actual latency profile of a given tool rather than treating every tool
   call as equivalent.
C) The fix is to remove the slow report-generation tool entirely, since any tool call
   taking more than a few seconds is inherently incompatible with a chat interface.
D) The fix is to reduce `max_tokens` on the report-generation call, since output
   length is what's causing the multi-minute duration rather than the report tool's
   own processing time.

### Q2.18 · Configuration Management
A team versions its model pins and prompt fragments in git, following good practice —
but also commits the production Anthropic API key directly into the same repository
alongside them, reasoning "it's all configuration, so it should all live in the same
place, version-controlled together."

A) This is sound practice, since version-controlling everything related to the
   application's behavior — including credentials — together in one place is always
   preferable to splitting it across systems.
B) Configuration (model versions, prompt content) and secrets (API keys, credentials)
   need different handling: the former benefits from version control, review, and
   history; the latter should never be committed to source control at all and belongs
   in a secrets manager loaded at runtime — treating both as "just configuration" and
   storing them identically creates a real credential-exposure risk.
C) API keys are safe to commit as long as the repository itself is private, since
   private-repository access is equivalent to a secrets manager's access control.
D) This is only a concern for public repositories; a key committed to any
   private repository carries no meaningful exposure risk under any circumstance.

### Q2.19 · Configuration Management *(select TWO)*
Which two practices reflect sound handling of environment-specific configuration
(dev/staging/production) for a Claude-based application?

A) Externalize environment-specific values (model pin, feature flags, endpoint
   targets) into environment-specific configuration rather than hardcoding a single
   environment's values directly into application code.
B) Keep the exact same hardcoded configuration values across dev, staging, and
   production, since using identical values everywhere guarantees consistent
   behavior regardless of environment-specific needs.
C) Make it clear, at a glance, which configuration is active in a given environment
   (e.g., via a manifest or environment-scoped config source), so a deploy to the
   wrong environment with the wrong configuration is easy to catch rather than
   discovered only after something breaks.
D) Store each environment's configuration only in the memory of whichever engineer
   last touched that environment, since environment-specific tribal knowledge is
   sufficiently reliable for a small team.
E) Avoid distinguishing environments at all, running dev, staging, and production
   against the exact same configuration source simultaneously, since a single shared
   source is inherently simpler to reason about.

