## Domain 3: Claude Code (Q3.1–Q3.2)

### Q3.1 · Claude Code Operation
An engineer wants a personal MCP server connection (access to their own local
database, used for their own ad hoc queries across whatever project they're working
in) to be available to them specifically, everywhere. Separately, a team wants an MCP
server connection to a shared internal service available to every engineer working
in one specific repository, regardless of who they are.

A) Both should be configured identically at the project level in that repository's
   `.mcp.json`, since project-level configuration is the only scope Claude Code
   supports for MCP servers.
B) The personal database connection fits user-level MCP configuration (follows the
   engineer across whatever project they work in); the shared internal service
   connection fits project-level MCP configuration in that specific repository (available
   to everyone working there, regardless of who they are) — different audiences call
   for different scopes.
C) Both should be configured at the user level, since MCP server connections are
   inherently personal and cannot be meaningfully shared across a team.
D) Neither can be configured through Claude Code's own configuration mechanisms; both
   would require a fully custom integration built outside Claude Code entirely.

### Q3.2 · Claude Code Operation
A team wants Claude Code to execute trivial, unambiguous single-file edits (a typo
fix, a version bump) directly without friction, but wants a genuinely risky,
multi-file operation — such as a production database migration script — to require
an explicit plan and approval step before any file is touched.

A) Plan mode (or an equivalent approval gate) should be reserved for the kind of
   task where surfacing a plan and getting alignment before acting actually adds
   value — the risky, hard-to-reverse migration — rather than applied uniformly to
   every edit regardless of its actual stakes, which would add friction to the
   trivial cases without a corresponding safety benefit.
B) Plan mode should be applied to every single edit without exception, since more
   review is always safer regardless of how trivial or reversible a given change is.
C) Plan mode should be disabled entirely across the board, since the friction observed
   on trivial edits shows that plan mode provides no genuine value for any task.
D) The distinction between trivial and risky changes should be enforced exclusively
   through code review after the fact, since a pre-execution gate can never usefully
   distinguish between the two.

