## Domain 5: Model Selection and Optimization (Q5.1–Q5.10)

### Q5.1 · LLM Fundamentals
A team needs the model to stop generating immediately before a specific delimiter
string, for downstream parsing convenience, and currently lets the model generate
freely, then manually searches the full output for the delimiter and truncates
everything after it in post-processing.

A) The manual post-processing approach is the only option, since there's no request
   parameter that can influence where generation itself stops.
B) A custom stop sequence (`stop_sequences`) can be configured so the model stops
   generating exactly at the chosen delimiter, addressing the need directly at
   generation time rather than requiring the client to generate extra content and
   then search and truncate after the fact.
C) The fix is to lower the temperature to 0, since deterministic sampling causes the
   model to naturally stop exactly at any delimiter present in the prompt.
D) The fix is to reduce `max_tokens` to a value just under where the delimiter
   typically appears, since output-length limiting is functionally equivalent to a
   stop sequence for this purpose.

### Q5.2 · LLM Fundamentals
A team estimates a prompt's token count by counting characters and dividing by four
as a rough rule of thumb, and is repeatedly surprised when requests near their
estimated limit are rejected as exceeding the model's actual token budget by a
meaningful margin.

A) This is expected and cannot be improved, since token count can never be estimated
   more precisely than a rough character-based approximation regardless of the
   method used.
B) Using an actual token-counting utility or endpoint (rather than a rough
   character-based approximation) gives a materially more accurate estimate before
   sending a request, since tokenization doesn't map to characters at any fixed,
   reliable ratio across all text.
C) The fix is to always assume the maximum possible token count for any input
   regardless of its actual length, since overestimating is the only way to avoid
   this class of error.
D) The character-to-token ratio is fixed and identical across every language and
   content type, so the team's estimate should already have been accurate; the
   rejection must indicate an unrelated issue.

### Q5.3 · LLM Fundamentals
A workflow reads small, sometimes slightly blurry printed values from photographed
receipts and passes whatever Claude reports directly into an accounting system with
no verification step, on the assumption that vision-based extraction of small or
imperfect text is essentially always accurate.

A) This assumption is reasonable to build on directly, since vision-based text
   extraction should be treated as effectively perfect regardless of image quality
   or text size.
B) Vision-based extraction, like any model capability, has real accuracy limits —
   especially on small, blurry, or otherwise degraded source material — so passing
   extracted values straight into a system of record with no verification or
   plausibility check assumes a reliability the capability doesn't actually
   guarantee; a validation step (range checks, confidence signals, or spot-check
   escalation) fits this use case better than unconditional trust.
C) The fix is to disable vision entirely and require manual data entry for every
   receipt, since no verification strategy can meaningfully improve on manual entry
   for this use case.
D) The fix is to increase the model's context window, since a larger window would
   improve the model's ability to read small or blurry text within a single image.

### Q5.4 · Technical Fundamentals
A team hand-rolls a manual pagination loop to retrieve a large list-style result from
an API endpoint, tracking cursors and making repeated calls by hand, duplicating
functionality an official SDK's built-in auto-pagination helper already provides for
exactly this kind of endpoint.

A) Custom pagination logic is always preferable to an SDK's built-in helper, since
   full manual control over each page request is inherently more valuable than
   convenience regardless of what the SDK already provides.
B) Where the SDK already provides auto-pagination for the relevant endpoint,
   reimplementing the same cursor-tracking logic by hand typically costs more to
   build and maintain correctly than adopting the SDK's helper — custom pagination
   logic earns its cost when the built-in helper doesn't fit a genuinely different
   need.
C) SDKs never provide any pagination-related functionality, so hand-rolling cursor
   logic is the only way to retrieve a paginated result at all.
D) The choice between custom and SDK-provided pagination has no bearing on
   correctness, only on the number of lines of code involved.

### Q5.5 · Technical Fundamentals
Several internal services each independently implement their own retry logic, request
logging, and response caching around their calls to the Claude API, with each
implementation slightly different and each team maintaining its own copy of
essentially the same cross-cutting logic.

A) This is optimal, since each service having full independent control over its own
   retry, logging, and caching logic is always preferable to any shared approach
   regardless of how much duplication results.
B) Centralizing these cross-cutting concerns (rate-limiting, logging, and caching)
   behind an internal API gateway or proxy in front of the Anthropic API would let
   every service benefit from one well-maintained implementation instead of each team
   separately building, maintaining, and inevitably diverging on the same logic.
C) The correct fix is to have every service call the Claude API directly with zero
   retry, logging, or caching logic of any kind, since removing the duplicated logic
   entirely is simpler than centralizing it.
D) This pattern has no meaningful cost, since duplicated cross-cutting logic across
   services never diverges in practice regardless of how many independent teams
   maintain their own copy.

### Q5.6 · Technical Fundamentals
A developer, evaluating how to integrate with the Claude API, assumes it's exposed as
a gRPC service and begins designing a gRPC client and protocol-buffer schema to
communicate with it.

A) This is correct; the Claude API is exposed exclusively via gRPC, so a gRPC client
   is the only way to integrate with it directly.
B) The Claude API is a REST/HTTPS-based API (with streaming support via server-sent
   events, and additional access patterns like websockets in certain client
   contexts) — not a gRPC service — so designing a gRPC client and protobuf schema
   against it is based on an incorrect premise about the underlying protocol.
C) gRPC and REST are interchangeable terms for the same underlying protocol, so the
   developer's approach will work identically regardless of which term is used.
D) The Claude API supports gRPC only for the Batches API specifically, while the
   Messages API remains REST-based, so a hybrid client would be required to
   support both endpoints correctly.

### Q5.7 · Model Selection and Tradeoffs
A team's documents are reliably short (well under 5,000 tokens), but the team defaults
every request to the model tier with the largest available context window "just in
case," even though a smaller-context, cheaper tier would comfortably handle every
document the application actually processes.

A) Defaulting to the largest available context window is always the correct choice
   regardless of actual document length, since more headroom can never be a
   disadvantage.
B) Matching model tier to the workload's actual context needs — rather than defaulting
   to the largest window "just in case" for documents that reliably fit comfortably
   within a smaller tier's window — avoids paying for headroom the application never
   actually uses.
C) Context window size has no bearing on model tier cost, so this decision carries no
   meaningful cost implication either way.
D) The fix is to artificially pad every document to consume the full available
   context window, since underutilizing an available window is itself a design flaw
   regardless of document length.

### Q5.8 · Model Selection and Tradeoffs
A team continues running production traffic against an older model snapshot well past
its published deprecation notice, taking no migration action until the model is
abruptly retired and the integration starts failing outright with no advance
warning the team had actually acted on.

A) This is an unavoidable outcome; deprecation notices provide no meaningful lead
   time and can't reasonably be planned around regardless of how far in advance they're
   published.
B) Tracking deprecation and retirement schedules for models in production use, and
   migrating proactively within the announced window, avoids exactly this kind of
   abrupt, unplanned outage — treating a published deprecation notice as actionable
   information rather than something to address only after retirement actually
   happens.
C) The correct response, now that retirement has already happened, is to request that
   Anthropic reverse the retirement, since deprecation schedules are always
   negotiable after the fact.
D) Deprecation notices only apply to workloads using extended thinking, so a workload
   not using that feature would never need to act on one regardless of which model
   it's pinned to.

### Q5.9 · Cost and Token Management
A team, wanting to reduce cost, cuts `max_tokens` sharply across every request type —
including ones whose responses were already, in practice, well under the previous
cap — and is confused why their actual monthly spend barely changes despite the much
lower ceiling.

A) This is expected: cost is driven by tokens actually generated, not by the
   `max_tokens` ceiling itself; lowering a cap that was already well above what
   responses naturally used doesn't reduce the tokens actually billed for those
   responses. Where `max_tokens` does matter for cost is capping the actual output
   in cases that would otherwise run long, so its cost impact depends on how close
   real generation was to the previous ceiling, not on the cap's raw value alone.
B) Lowering `max_tokens` always proportionally reduces cost by the same percentage the
   ceiling was reduced by, regardless of how much of the previous ceiling was
   actually being used by real responses.
C) `max_tokens` has no relationship to cost under any circumstance, so the team's
   entire approach was based on a false premise from the start.
D) The fix is to lower `max_tokens` to an extremely small value across every request
   type uniformly, since the smallest possible ceiling always minimizes cost
   regardless of whether it's large enough for the actual task.

### Q5.10 · Model Selection and Tradeoffs
A team is comparing two model tiers for a moderately complex extraction task and
bases its final decision entirely on one tier's slightly higher score on a widely
publicized general benchmark, without testing either tier against the team's own
representative sample of the actual documents the application will process.

A) This is sufficient, since a widely publicized general benchmark score is
   guaranteed to predict performance on any specific application's actual document
   distribution.
B) A benchmark score reflects performance on a generic, published distribution that
   may or may not resemble this application's actual documents; testing both tiers
   against a representative sample of the real workload gives a more directly
   relevant signal for this specific decision than a general benchmark score alone.
C) Benchmark scores should be ignored entirely in every model-selection decision,
   since only live production data is ever a valid input to the choice.
D) The two tiers are guaranteed to perform identically on this specific task
   regardless of any benchmark score difference, since benchmark scores never
   translate to any real-world difference in output quality.

