## Domain 6: Prompt and Context Engineering (Q6.1–Q6.7)

### Q6.1 · Context Engineering
A single, very long document is placed in context, and the specific fact a downstream
question depends on happens to sit in the middle of that document. The model's
answer misses or garbles that fact more often than facts located near the start or
end of the same document, even though the fact itself is stated clearly in the
source text.

A) This is unrelated to context engineering and indicates a comprehension limitation
   that no structural change to the prompt could address.
B) This reflects a known pattern where information in the middle of a long context
   can be attended to less reliably than information near the start or end;
   mitigations include placing critical information where it's more reliably
   attended to, chunking and retrieving only the relevant section rather than
   supplying the entire document, or explicitly directing attention to the relevant
   part rather than assuming any position within a large blob is retrieved equally
   well.
C) The fix is exclusively to increase the model's context window, since a larger
   window eliminates any positional effect on how reliably content is retrieved
   within it.
D) The fix is to repeat the entire document twice in the prompt, since duplicating
   the full content guarantees the fact will be found regardless of its position
   either time.

### Q6.2 · Context Engineering
An agent's harness re-sends a large, identical block of reference material (a
product catalog) with every single turn of a long conversation, even on turns where
the user's question has nothing to do with the catalog at all, because removing it
entirely from any turn "feels risky."

A) Always including the full catalog regardless of relevance is the safest approach,
   since there's no way to know in advance which turns might need it.
B) A just-in-time or conditional-inclusion pattern — including the catalog only on
   turns where it's actually relevant to the question being asked, rather than
   unconditionally on every turn regardless of topic — avoids paying the context
   cost of irrelevant reference material on turns that don't need it at all.
C) The fix is to remove the catalog from context permanently and rely on the model's
   general training knowledge of typical product catalogs instead.
D) The fix is to shorten the catalog by half uniformly, since a smaller version
   included on every turn is preferable to a full version included only when
   relevant.

### Q6.3 · Prompt Engineering
A team writes a system prompt containing a firm behavioral rule ("never disclose
internal pricing formulas") and, several paragraphs later in the same prompt, a
softer, more general instruction ("be as helpful and transparent as possible") that
can be read as being in tension with the firm rule in an edge case where full
transparency would require disclosing exactly the kind of formula the rule
prohibits.

A) This tension is harmless and will never surface in practice, since a capable
   model will always resolve any two instructions consistently regardless of how
   they're phrased or where they sit in the prompt.
B) Instructions that can conflict in a specific edge case should be explicitly
   reconciled by the prompt author (e.g., stating precedence, or narrowing the softer
   instruction's scope so it doesn't reach the case the firm rule covers) rather than
   left for the model to resolve silently and unpredictably case by case.
C) The fix is to remove the firm rule entirely and rely solely on the general
   helpfulness instruction, since a single instruction can never conflict with
   itself.
D) The fix is to repeat the firm rule ten times throughout the prompt, since sheer
   repetition count is what determines which of two competing instructions wins in
   an edge case.

### Q6.4 · Prompt Engineering
A team wants a classification prompt to reliably distinguish between two genuinely
similar categories that differ on a subtle criterion, and currently only describes
the criterion in prose, with no worked example showing a case near the actual
boundary between the two categories.

A) A prose description alone is always sufficient for any classification distinction,
   regardless of how subtle or boundary-adjacent the categories are.
B) Adding a worked example specifically near the boundary between the two similar
   categories — not just clearly-typical examples of each — gives the model a
   concrete demonstration of exactly the distinction that matters, which a purely
   prose-based description of a subtle criterion may not convey as reliably.
C) The fix is to merge the two similar categories into one, since any distinction
   subtle enough to need a boundary example is too fine-grained to be worth
   preserving.
D) The fix is to increase temperature so the model considers a wider range of
   possible categories before settling on one.

### Q6.5 · Prompt Engineering
A team's prompt instructs the model to "format your response as JSON" but never
specifies the exact schema, key names, or nesting structure expected, and downstream
parsing breaks whenever the model's JSON, though syntactically valid, uses
differently-named keys or a different structure than what the parser expects from
one response to the next.

A) This is unavoidable, since JSON output can never be made more consistent than
   whatever variation naturally occurs, regardless of what the prompt specifies.
B) Specifying the exact expected schema (key names, types, nesting) explicitly in
   the prompt — or better, using a mechanism that enforces structure at the API level
   — gives the model (and the downstream parser) a fixed target, rather than leaving
   "format as JSON" to be interpreted differently from response to response.
C) The fix is to abandon structured output entirely and parse free-form prose with
   regular expressions instead, since prose is inherently easier to parse reliably
   than any JSON schema.
D) The fix is to lower the temperature to 0, since deterministic sampling guarantees
   identical key names and structure across every response regardless of what the
   prompt specifies.

### Q6.6 · Output Handling
A pipeline receives a tool call's numeric output and passes it directly into a
financial calculation with no check on whether the value's sign is plausible for the
field it represents (e.g., a negative value in a field that should only ever be a
non-negative quantity), because the schema only constrains the field to be a
`number`, not to any specific range or sign.

A) Schema-level type constraints (`number`) are sufficient on their own; no
   additional check is needed once a value's basic type has been validated.
B) Defensive parsing beyond bare type-checking — sign and range plausibility checks
   appropriate to what a given field actually represents — catches technically
   type-valid but semantically implausible values a bare `number` constraint doesn't
   rule out, before they reach a downstream calculation.
C) The fix is to change the field's type to `string` instead of `number`, since
   string fields are inherently less prone to implausible values than numeric ones.
D) The fix is to reject any response containing a negative number anywhere in it,
   regardless of whether that specific field should ever legitimately be negative.

### Q6.7 · Output Handling *(select TWO)*
A legal-document-review feature produces a confident-sounding risk assessment
("this clause poses low risk") for each contract clause reviewed, and staff
currently treat every assessment as a final determination with no further review.
Which two practices most directly reduce the risk of an incorrect risk assessment
being acted on as if it were a verified conclusion?

A) Treat the assessment's confident phrasing as no evidence of its actual
   correctness — a fluently stated "low risk" carries no more inherent reliability
   than a hedged one, and the two should not be trusted differently based on tone
   alone.
B) Surface the specific clause language and reasoning behind each assessment
   alongside the verdict, so a reviewer can check the actual basis for a "low risk"
   or "high risk" call rather than accepting the label alone as sufficient
   justification.
C) Increase the temperature so risk assessments are phrased with more visible
   hedging, since varied phrasing itself functions as a reliable calibration signal.
D) Assume that because the assessments are for internal use only rather than
   external distribution, no verification step is necessary regardless of what
   staff do with the output.
E) Add more few-shot examples of confidently-phrased "low risk" assessments so future
   assessments sound even more authoritative.

