## Domain 8: Tools and MCPs (Q8.1–Q8.7)

### Q8.1 · Tool Implementation
A `send_email` tool's description says only "sends an email." The agent, given a
request to "let the customer know about the delay," sometimes calls it with an
overly casual tone inappropriate for the situation, and sometimes fails to include
necessary details like a revised delivery estimate, with no guidance in the
description about what makes a well-formed call for this specific tool.

A) The tool is fine as designed, since a one-line description is sufficient for any
   tool regardless of how much judgment the model needs to exercise when
   constructing its arguments.
B) Expanding the description to clarify what a well-formed call looks like for this
   tool — expected tone, what information a delay notification should include — gives
   the model the guidance it needs to construct better-formed calls, rather than
   leaving those judgment calls entirely to inference from a bare one-line
   description.
C) The fix is to remove the tool entirely and have the agent draft emails as plain
   text in its response instead, bypassing the need for any tool description at all.
D) The fix is to add more unrelated example tools to the agent's toolset, since a
   richer overall toolset improves description quality for every individual tool.

### Q8.2 · Tool Implementation
A `cancel_subscription` tool executes immediately and irreversibly whenever called,
with no distinction in how it's dispatched between a user explicitly confirming
"yes, cancel my subscription now" in the conversation versus the agent inferring,
from an ambiguous remark like "I've been thinking about my subscriptions lately,"
that cancellation is probably what the user wants.

A) This is fine, since a well-designed schema for the tool's parameters is sufficient
   regardless of how confidently the decision to call it was actually reached.
B) An irreversible action like this warrants a dispatch/approval pattern that
   accounts for how confidently the intent was actually established — e.g., requiring
   an explicit, unambiguous confirmation before executing, rather than treating an
   inferred, ambiguous signal as equivalent grounds for calling the same irreversible
   tool.
C) The fix is exclusively to rewrite the tool's description in more detail, since
   dispatch-confidence concerns are always resolved by better documentation alone.
D) The fix is to remove the tool from the agent's toolset entirely, foregoing the
   capability rather than building an appropriate confirmation gate for it.

### Q8.3 · Tool Implementation
A team builds ten separate, narrowly-scoped tools (`get_order_status`,
`get_order_total`, `get_order_items`, `get_order_address`, and so on) that each
return one small piece of the same underlying order record, forcing the agent to
make several separate calls and reason about combining their results for almost
every order-related question, when a single well-designed `get_order` tool
returning the structured full record would serve nearly every such question in one
call.

A) Ten narrowly-scoped tools are always preferable to one broader tool, since more
   granular tools are inherently more maintainable regardless of how often a task
   actually needs to combine several of them.
B) Tool set construction should weigh how a toolset is actually used: when almost
   every real task needs to combine several narrow tools' results, consolidating
   them into one well-designed tool returning the full relevant record often serves
   the actual usage pattern better than forcing repeated multi-call assembly for
   nearly every question.
C) The fix is to add a tool that calls all ten other tools internally, adding an
   eleventh tool on top of the original ten rather than reconsidering the original
   decomposition.
D) Tool granularity has no bearing on agent performance or efficiency, so the
   current design and a single consolidated tool would perform identically in
   practice.

### Q8.4 · MCP Server Development
A team builds an MCP server and, during development, is deciding between stdio
transport and a network transport (like streamable HTTP) purely based on "which one
is newer," without considering that the server is meant to be run as a local
subprocess of a single desktop client with no network exposure at all, now or in any
currently planned future use.

A) The newer transport is always the correct choice regardless of the server's
   actual intended deployment, since newer generally implies better in every
   dimension relevant to this decision.
B) Transport choice should follow the server's actual deployment shape: a
   single-client local subprocess with no network exposure fits stdio well, without
   needing the additional complexity a network transport introduces for a deployment
   that was never going to need network reachability in the first place.
C) stdio is deprecated and should never be used for any new MCP server regardless of
   deployment context, since network transports have fully superseded it in every
   use case.
D) Transport choice has no bearing on implementation complexity or deployment fit;
   any transport can be swapped for any other with no practical consequence.

### Q8.5 · MCP Server Development
An MCP server exposes a `resources/list` capability returning hundreds of available
resources, but provides no way for a client to filter or search that list by
relevance to a specific task, forcing every client to retrieve and locally filter
the entire list on every connection just to find the handful of resources actually
relevant to what it's doing.

A) This is an acceptable server design, since any client capable of local filtering
   should be expected to handle the full unfiltered list regardless of how large it
   grows.
B) Designing the server's resource-listing capability to support more targeted
   retrieval (filtering, pagination, or logical grouping) as the resource count
   grows serves real client needs better than forcing every client to fetch and
   filter an ever-growing full list locally on every single connection.
C) The fix is to remove resources from the server entirely and expose everything as
   tools instead, since tools inherently scale better with large item counts than
   resources do.
D) List size has no bearing on server design quality, since network transfer cost is
   the only consideration relevant to how a resource list is exposed.

### Q8.6 · Agentic Customization
A team needs Claude to perform precise, multi-step mathematical calculations
involving many intermediate steps where even a small arithmetic error early on would
invalidate the final result, and is deciding whether to rely on the model's own
step-by-step reasoning entirely or to give it a calculator-style tool for the actual
arithmetic.

A) Relying entirely on the model's own reasoning for every arithmetic step is
   always preferable, since offloading calculation to a tool removes an opportunity
   to verify the model's reasoning process end to end.
B) Providing a tool for the actual arithmetic operations — letting the model
   reason about which calculations to perform while offloading the computation
   itself to something that executes it precisely — reduces the risk of a small
   compounding arithmetic error invalidating a multi-step result, which is exactly
   the kind of task where a tool's deterministic execution is a better fit than
   token-by-token generated arithmetic.
C) Tools should never be used for any task the model could in principle attempt on
   its own, regardless of how error-prone that path is for tasks with many
   compounding steps.
D) The choice between relying on reasoning alone and using a calculator tool has no
   bearing on the accuracy of a multi-step calculation, since accuracy depends
   entirely on prompt wording regardless of execution mechanism.

### Q8.7 · Agentic Customization
A team wants Claude to be able to check and update rows in a specific internal
spreadsheet-backed tracking system, used by several different internal Claude
applications, and is weighing a one-off custom tool built into a single
application against a reusable MCP server other applications could also connect to
— given that at least two other teams have already expressed interest in the same
capability for their own separate applications.

A) A one-off custom tool is always preferable, since building it once inside a single
   application is simpler than building a general-purpose server regardless of how
   many other teams want the same capability.
B) Given that multiple applications are known to want the same capability, an MCP
   server is the better fit here: it lets every interested application connect to
   one centrally maintained integration rather than each team separately building
   and maintaining its own copy of essentially the same tool.
C) Custom tools and MCP servers are strictly interchangeable in every respect, so the
   choice between them carries no practical consequence regardless of how many
   applications need the capability.
D) The fix is to have each of the three interested teams build their own
   independent custom tool, since independently-maintained implementations are
   inherently safer than one shared integration.

